hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2949","Narrated to us Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Ala and Sa'id ibn Abd al-Rahman, who both reported: \Narrated to us Sufyan"," from al-Zuhri"," from Isa ibn Talha"," from Abd Allah ibn Amr (RA)"," who said: A man came to the Prophet (ﷺ) on the Day of Nahr (the 10th of Dhul Hijjah"," the day of the Eid al-Adha sacrifice) and said"," 'I shaved my head before I performed my sacrifice.' The Prophet (ﷺ) replied"," 'Perform your sacrifice"," there is no blame upon you.' The man then said"," 'I performed my sacrifice before I threw stones at the Jamarat (the stone pillars in Mina).' The Prophet (ﷺ) said"," 'Throw the stones"," there is no blame upon you.' Al-Makhzumi (one of the narrators of this hadith) also stated in his own version of the narration: 'Indeed a man asked the Prophet (ﷺ)"," saying"," 'I shaved my head before I sacrificed","' and the Prophet (ﷺ) gave him the same permissive response. Then another man asked him"," saying"," 'I performed the nahr (ritual sacrifice"," typically of a camel) before I threw stones (at the Jamarat).'\""\nCommentary: This narration clarifies that if a pilgrim performs a ritual act of Hajj or the sacrificial rites of Eid al-Adha out of the prescribed order due to ignorance"," no sin is incurred"," and the individual is only required to complete the remaining rituals in accordance with prophetic guidance."""
"2950","Narrated to us Bishr ibn Mu‘ādh al-‘Aqadī and al-Sana‘ānī (may Allah have mercy on them), both said:\nNarrated to us Yazīd ibn Zuray‘, narrated to us Khālid, from ‘Ikrimah, from Ibn ‘Abbās (RA), who said:\nThe Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was asked on the Day of Sacrifice at Minā, and he would say: “There is no harm, there is no harm.”\nA man asked him and said: “I shaved my head before slaughtering.”\nHe (ﷺ) said: “There is no harm.”\nAnd he said: “I stoned the Jamarāt after nightfall.”\nHe (ﷺ) said: “There is no harm.”\n\nNarrated to us Naṣr ibn ‘Alī, informed us Yazīd ibn Zuray‘, with the same wording, and he added: “Slaughter and there is no harm.”\n\n**CHAPTER:**"
